OPEN - REDUCED ON SILICON AT HEAD, TWO HYPOTHESES REFUTED, MECHANISM NOT YET NAMED.
Not a finish-condition gate for this brief.

--- c-defects attempt 10, 2026-09-01 ---

D-C12: WITH THE ttnn PROGRAM CACHE WARM, ONLY THE FIRST DEVICE SAMPLING CALL IN A PROCESS
       RETURNS ITS OWN ANSWER. LATER CALLS RETURN AN EARLIER CALL'S.

WHY IT MATTERS HERE. It is what makes four of area 4's ten claim-verdicts fail - the seeded-slot
claim on both models (zq10-zq12, ze1-ze3) and, per attempt 9's correction, the T=2.0 half of the
near-zero-temperature test. D-C5 and D-C8 are fixed and their gate is met; this is what the
now-reachable assertions land on.

1. IT STILL REPRODUCES AT HEAD, and this had never been re-asked.
   The qualifying runs were taken 2026-08-29 at dc7f62430c0 - before the program-cache
   retirement (faec6e59938) and before close() released weights (299440bb276). Re-asked at
   c27f187e5596, three fresh processes, 17.89 / 18.06 / 17.85 s
   (logs/dc12_repeat_head_r{1,2,3}.log). All three [repeat] blocks are BYTE-IDENTICAL
   (md5 42d42591e9d7becf580287d3143b94f9 over every `[repeat]` line, all three runs):

     cache cleared before each call : call 0,1,2,3 all wrong=0/32   -> "does not reproduce here"
     program cache warm            : call 0 wrong=0/32
                                     call 1 wrong=32/32  expected 1, got 0
                                     call 2 wrong=32/32  expected 2, got 0
                                     call 3 wrong=32/32  expected 3, got 1
   Every returned id is IN vocabulary (out-of-vocab=0), so this is a stale ANSWER, not garbage.

2. IT IS NOT A PREMATURE READBACK. Attempt 9's hypothesis was that on a cache hit there is no
   compile to stall the host, so the read races the still-running program. The bisect reads the
   same output THREE times per call - read1 immediately as production does, read2 after
   `ttnn.synchronize_device`, read3 after `reset_sub_device_stall_group()` and a synchronize:

     mode=cache, every call, every run:   read1==read2 True   read2==read3 True

   Twelve of twelve observations across three fresh processes. A race would show read1 != read2.
   **The hypothesis is REFUTED and attempt 9's section 9 is withdrawn on this point.** The stale
   value is committed in the buffer; no amount of synchronizing at the read changes it.

3. IT IS NOT A MOVED ADDRESS. The bisect records every one of the thirteen ops' output buffer
   addresses per call. Between call 0 and calls 1/2/3, in ALL THREE modes:

     op00 from_torch   op04 interleaved_to_sharded  op05 topk    op06 all_gather
     op07 all_gather   op08 typecast                op09 add     op10 untilize
     op11 manual_seed  op12 sampling                              -> address SAME, every time
     op01 sharded_to_interleaved, op02 matmul, op03 add           -> address DIFFERS

   The three that move, move identically in the mode that produces CORRECT results, so address
   movement is not the discriminator. The received "an address baked as a plain uint32 in cached
   runtime args" story is refuted as the mechanism, which confirms attempt 9's negative source
   reading (reduction/{topk,sampling,manual_seed} have no address() call between them).

4. WHAT DOES DISCRIMINATE: A HOST READBACK BETWEEN THE OPS. mode=cache_sig is mode=cache plus
   one host read of each intermediate between ops - nothing else differs, same cache, same
   addresses. It is CORRECT in all four calls, three fresh processes:

     mode=nocache   wrong_by_call=[[], [], [], []]
     mode=cache     wrong_by_call=[[], [all 32], [all 32], [all 32]]
     mode=cache_sig wrong_by_call=[[], [], [], []]

   So observing the chain fixes it. The defect is sensitive to host/device synchronization
   inserted BETWEEN the ops, and not to synchronization at the final read (point 2). That is the
   signature of a missing dependency between two ops on a cache hit, not of a stale read.

5. THE LAG IS NOT FIXED, and that is a clue rather than noise. r1 and r2 are byte-identical over
   every `[bisect]` line (md5 e51eb22af29004593bd5caf645055ac7). r3 differs in exactly one cell:
   call 3 returned call 2's answer ([2, 4750, ...]) where r1/r2 returned call 1's
   ([1, 4749, ...]). So the PATTERN is deterministic 3/3 and the DEPTH of the lag varies. A
   single stale buffer would lag by exactly one, always.

6. ONE OBSERVATION TO HAND ON, not yet interpreted. `op11 manual_seed` and `op12 sampling` are
   reported at the SAME output address (2990304) in every mode and every call. If those two
   genuinely share a buffer, then the ordering between them is the dependency most likely to be
   lost on a cache hit - and point 4 says a dependency is exactly what is missing. This is a
   pointer for whoever picks it up, not a conclusion: the probe did not test it.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT (not this job's - the ops are ttnn's, not shared Galaxy code):
  a) run mode=cache with a bare `ttnn.synchronize_device()` between ops and NO readback. If that
     alone fixes it, the missing thing is a barrier; if only the readback fixes it, it is the
     buffer transfer, which is narrower.
  b) bisect by inserting the sync at ONE op boundary at a time - thirteen 20-second arms - to
     name the boundary. That is the cheapest remaining experiment on this defect and it has not
     been run.

WHAT WAS NOT DONE. Nothing was relaxed and no test was xfailed. The four area-4 verdicts that
fail because of this are recorded as failures in the gate ledger.

MEANWHILE, FOR CALLERS: `disable_and_clear_program_cache()` before each sampling call is correct
and measured (mode=nocache, 4/4 calls, three fresh processes). It costs a full recompile per
call, so it is a diagnosis, not a fix.

Logs: logs/dc12_repeat_head_r{1,2,3}.log, logs/dc12_bisect_r{1,2,3}.log
Probe (DIAGNOSTIC ONLY, never committed): tttv2_dc12_scratch/test_dc12_op_bisect.py
